<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Patch>

  <!-- ===========================================================================================
       Puts barn owls into the wild animal tables.

       Tyto alba has one of the widest natural ranges of any land bird, which argues for adding it
       nearly everywhere. It is held back from that here for two reasons. Ice sheet, sea ice, tundra,
       cold bog and extreme desert are out because there is nothing for a small predator to hunt.
       Tropical rainforest is out because barn owls are birds of open country and hunt on the wing
       over ground they can see.

       Weights run 0.1 to 0.3, and the ceiling is the part worth explaining. Vanilla prices every
       predator in the game at 0.07 without exception: wolf, fox, cougar, lynx, grizzly and warg all
       carry that same number in every biome they appear in. Non-predator birds sit at 0.5. This is a
       predator, so it belongs near the low end rather than the high one, and the margin it does get
       over 0.07 is earned by maxPreyBodySize 0.15, which means it cannot threaten a colonist, a
       muffalo, or anything else that matters. A wolf is kept rare because it is dangerous; an owl
       is not, so it can be three or four times as common without changing what a map feels like.

       Within that band the ranking is habitat. Open country ranks highest, cover and hard ground
       lowest.

       Everything past the Core block is gated. MayRequire on a list element is checked against
       active packageIds at load, so an operation naming a mod that is not installed is skipped and
       nothing errors. MayRequireAnyOf covers the case where two mods can supply the same biome.
       PatchOperationFindMod would also work, but it matches on mod name rather than packageId,
       which is the looser of the two.

       The Core block is not conditional: every biome named there is Core and always present. A
       PatchOperationAdd that finds nothing logs an error, which is the right outcome, because it
       would mean the game renamed a biome and this patch needs revisiting.
       =========================================================================================== -->

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationSequence">
    <success>Normal</success>
    <operations>

      <!-- Core: open country with prey in it. The best habitats. -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="TemperateForest"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.2</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="AridShrubland"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.2</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="TemperateSwamp"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.15</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <!-- Core: thinner ground. Present, but you will not trip over one. -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="BorealForest"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.1</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="Desert"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.1</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="TropicalSwamp"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.1</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <!-- Odyssey.

           Grasslands is the top of the band at 0.3. Open grass with hedgerows is the signature barn
           owl habitat, and it is the one biome where this should be the bird you half expect rather
           than the one you notice. Glacial plain, lava field, Scarlands and orbit are left out for
           the same reason as the Core exclusions: nothing there for a small predator to hunt.

           Glowforest sits at the floor because it is dense cover, and an owl hunts on the wing over
           ground it can see. -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd" MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Odyssey">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="Grasslands"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.3</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd" MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Odyssey">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="Glowforest"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.1</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <!-- ReGrowth: Aspen. Open aspen woodland, with hare and squirrel at 1.0 and a red fox at the
           usual 0.07, so it reads as temperate forest and is priced as one. -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd" MayRequire="ReGrowth.BOTR.AspenForest">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="RG_AspenForest"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.2</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <!-- Gulden Biome. A temperate forest variant with its own prey list, including Gulden_Rat and
           the rabbitgaroo, both at 1.0. Same habitat, same weight as temperate forest. -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd" MayRequire="pphhyy.GuldenNew">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="TemperateGuldenForest"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.2</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <!-- ReGrowth: Boiling. Forest, and hot, so it sits at the floor for the same reason boreal
           does: prey is there, but it is cover rather than open hunting ground. -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd" MayRequire="ReGrowth.BOTR.BoilingForest">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="RG_BoilingForest"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.1</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <!-- Dark Forest. Supplied by either Standalone Dark Forest or the full Medieval Overhaul, and
           the biome defName is the same in both, so this uses MayRequireAnyOf rather than two
           operations that would collide when both mods are on. Dense cover, so the floor. -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd"
          MayRequireAnyOf="DankPyon.Medieval.DarkForest,DankPyon.Medieval.Overhaul">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="DankPyon_DarkForest"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.1</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <!-- Lightless Empyrean.

           A pale, ghost-faced bird belongs in a lightless realm, and that is the whole argument for
           this one, because the ecological argument runs the other way: the biome's table is
           predators and undead, with no hare, squirrel or rat anywhere in it. Nothing there is under
           maxPreyBodySize for an owl to hunt.

           That is deliberate rather than an oversight, and it is not a starvation sentence either.
           The race is CarnivoreAnimal, so a wild owl here lives off corpses, and a realm like this
           one supplies them. It simply will not hunt, which is a fair trade for the picture. -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd" MayRequire="pphhyy.lightlessempyrean">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="pphhyy_LightlessEmpyrean_Biome"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl>0.15</BarnOwl>
        </value>
      </li>

      <!-- Duskwood, and the one biome that gets a different bird.

           BarnOwl_Dusk rather than BarnOwl: a biome table keys on PawnKindDef, so the only way to
           say "the grey ones here" is a kind whose graphic pool is already only the grey ones. See
           the def file for what that kind is, and for why sharing the race is safe.

           At 0.15 rather than the floor, because this mod prices its own cursed wolves and bears at
           0.14, twice what vanilla gives a predator. An owl at the floor would vanish next to
           them. -->

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd" MayRequire="Okagrim.duskwood">
        <xpath>Defs/BiomeDef[defName="Duskwood"]/wildAnimals</xpath>
        <value>
          <BarnOwl_Dusk>0.15</BarnOwl_Dusk>
        </value>
      </li>

    </operations>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
